title: "Generative UI Development Services | Zunkiree Labs" # SEOAI:TITLE
description: "Generative UI development: interfaces assembled at runtime from your design system, where the model picks and fills real components users can act on."
permalink: /solutions/generative-ui/
hideChrome: true
bodyClass: "v2-inter"
faqs:
  - question: "What is generative UI, in one sentence?"
    answer: "It is an interface composed at runtime by a model choosing and filling components from your design system, rather than a set of screens laid out in advance by a developer."
  - question: "How is this different from a chatbot in our product?"
    answer: "A chatbot returns text and leaves the user to go and do the thing. Generative UI returns the thing — the populated form, the chart with its filters, the button that completes the action. The conversation is the input method; the output is still your product's interface."
  - question: "Can the model invent components or break our design system?"
    answer: "No, and that is the point of the architecture rather than a promise about model behaviour. It can only emit a component name from a registry you define, with props a schema validates. An unknown name or an invalid prop fails closed and falls back to text, so the worst case is a plain answer, never broken or off-brand markup."
  - question: "Does every screen in our product need rebuilding?"
    answer: "No — and it would be a poor idea. Fixed screens are better wherever the task is well understood and repeated, which is most of a mature product. Generative surfaces earn their place on the long tail: the questions nobody anticipated and the one-off views that never justified a build. We usually start with a single surface alongside your existing UI."
  - question: "What about latency — will users wait for an interface to generate?"
    answer: "Components stream as they resolve rather than appearing all at once at the end, so the first useful element lands quickly. Where a task is predictable we cache the arrangement and regenerate only the data, which removes model latency from the common path entirely."
  - question: "How do you test an interface that is different every time?"
    answer: "You test the pieces and the contract, not the permutations. The components have their own tests as usual; the model's output is validated against the prop schemas; and we keep a suite of recorded real requests as regression cases. What you cannot do is rely on screenshot comparison of whole pages, which is the genuine cost of the approach."
